  ("That is, the procedure modifies the tableau in such a way that the disjunction of" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1243 0.1377 0.1377 0.0134 0.00731 0.2313 0.0415 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 14)
  ("the formulas represented by the leaf nodes of the resulting tableau is equivalent to" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1439 0.1573 0.1573 0.0134 0.00652 0.2297 0.0266 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("the original one. One of these conjunctions may contain a pair of complementary" 0.1812 0.8731 0.1635 0.1769 0.1769 0.0134 0.00719 0.2651 0.0266 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 12)
  ("literals, in which case that conjunction is equivalent to false. If all conjunctions are" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1831 0.1965 0.1965 0.0134 0.00594 0.2542 0.0617 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("equivalent to false, the original formula is unsatisfiable. This shows the soundness" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2028 0.2162 0.2162 0.0134 0.00682 0.2583 0.0845 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 11)
  ("of the procedure." 0.1812 0.3236 0.2224 0.2358 0.2358 0.0134 0.00637 0.1874 0.0154 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 2)
  ("If a formula is unsatisfiable, then every term in its DNF must be equivalent" 0.2282 0.8730 0.2420 0.2554 0.2554 0.0134 0.00719 0.2861 0.0127 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("to false and the corresponding node in the tableau is closed. Thus we will have a" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2616 0.2750 0.2750 0.0134 0.00715 0.2353 0.0170 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 15)
  ("closed tableau. This shows the completeness of the procedure." 0.1812 0.7020 0.2812 0.2947 0.2947 0.0134 0.00637 0.2496 0.0500 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 8)
  ("Since neither α-rules nor β-rules can be used forever (each rule reduces ei-" 0.2282 0.8729 0.3009 0.3143 0.3143 0.0134 0.00824 0.1895 0.0436 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 12)
  ("ther one occurrence of a binary operator or two negations), the procedure must be" 0.1812 0.8731 0.3205 0.3339 0.3339 0.0134 0.00641 0.2196 0.0340 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("terminating." 0.1812 0.2843 0.3401 0.3535 0.3535 0.0134 nil 0.3194 0.1031 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 0)
  ("2" 0.8583 0.8729 0.3385 0.3536 0.3536 0.0151 nil nil 0.0146 "EYIZNG+LASY10" 0.0151 nil t 0)
  ("Of course, semantic tableau can be used to show the satisfiability of a formula." 0.2282 0.8730 0.3688 0.3822 0.3822 0.0134 0.0051 0.3115 0.0207 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("If there is a model node in the tableau, we may assign 1 to every literal in this node" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3885 0.4019 0.4019 0.0134 0.00561 0.2967 0.0127 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 18)
  ("so that the formula represented by this node is true under this interpretation. The" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4081 0.4215 0.4215 0.0134 0.00629 0.2397 0.0171 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("original formula will be true under this interpretation because it is equivalent to the" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4277 0.4411 0.4411 0.0134 0.00537 0.2828 0.0627 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("disjunction of the formulas of the leaf nodes. Thus, this interpretation is a model" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4473 0.4607 0.4607 0.0134 0.00709 0.2625 0.0920 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("of the original formula. This shows that the procedure can be used to find a model" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4669 0.4803 0.4803 0.0134 0.00608 0.2705 0.0154 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 15)
  ("when the formula is satisfiable." 0.1812 0.4408 0.4866 0.5000 0.5000 0.0134 0.00637 0.2889 0.0436 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 4)
  ("Semantic tableau specifies what rules to be used but it does not specify which" 0.2282 0.8730 0.5062 0.5196 0.5196 0.0134 0.00565 0.2761 0.0760 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("rule should be used first or which node should be expanded first. In general, α-rules" 0.1812 0.8729 0.5258 0.5392 0.5392 0.0134 0.00539 0.2625 0.0319 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 14)
  ("are better be applied before β-rules, so that the number of nodes in a tree can be" 0.1812 0.8730 0.5454 0.5588 0.5588 0.0134 0.00693 0.2217 0.0255 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 16)
  ("reduced. In practice, the order of formulas in a list can be changed and the order" 0.1812 0.8730 0.5651 0.5785 0.5785 0.0134 0.00697 0.2203 0.0701 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 15)
  ("of β_{1} and β_{2} can be switched. This gives the user the freedom to construct trees of" 0.1812 0.8730 0.5847 0.5994 0.5981 0.0134 0.00623 0.2439 0.0154 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 16)
  ("different shapes, or to find a model node quicker. The procedure may terminate once" 0.1812 0.8730 0.6043 0.6177 0.6177 0.0134 0.00461 0.3080 0.0691 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("a model node is found, if the goal is to show that the original formula is satisfiable." 0.1812 0.8730 0.6239 0.6373 0.6373 0.0134 0.00596 0.2917 0.0096 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 16)
  ("Semantic tableaux are much more expressive and easy to use than truth-tables," 0.2282 0.8730 0.6436 0.6570 0.6570 0.0134 0.00445 0.3007 0.0760 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 11)
  ("though that is not the reason for their introduction. Sometimes, a tableau uses more" 0.1812 0.8731 0.6632 0.6766 0.6766 0.0134 0.00481 0.3041 0.0585 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("space than a truth table. For example, an unsatisfiable full CNF of n variables will" 0.1812 0.8729 0.6828 0.6962 0.6962 0.0134 0.006 0.2905 0.0447 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 14)
  ("generate a tableau of more than n! nodes, which is large than a truth table of 2^{n}" 0.1812 0.8721 0.7005 0.7158 0.7158 0.0134 0.00731 0.2258 0.0702 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 16)
  ("lines. The beauty of semantic tableaux lies on the simplicity of presenting a proof" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7220 0.7355 0.7355 0.0134 0.00676 0.2677 0.0426 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("procedure using a set of α and β rules. We will see in later chapters how new" 0.1812 0.8729 0.7417 0.7551 0.7551 0.0134 0.00871 0.2493 0.0824 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 16)
