  ("Both of semantic tableaux and Hilbert systems for constructing proofs have" 0.2282 0.8730 0.1458 0.1592 0.1592 0.0134 0.00736 0.2494 0.0412 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 10)
  ("their disadvantages." 0.1812 0.3499 0.1654 0.1788 0.1788 0.0134 0.00906 0.1822 0.0393 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 1)
  ("Hilbert’s systems are difficult to construct proofs in; their" 0.3665 0.8730 0.1654 0.1788 0.1788 0.0134 0.00906 0.2663 0.0724 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 8)
  ("main uses are metalogical, the small number of rules making it easier to prove results" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1850 0.1984 0.1984 0.0134 0.00463 0.3487 0.0415 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 14)
  ("about logic. The tableau method on the other hand is easy to use mechanically but," 0.1812 0.8730 0.2046 0.2180 0.2180 0.0134 0.00527 0.2854 0.0484 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 14)
  ("because of the form of the connective rules and the fact that a tableau starts from" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2243 0.2377 0.2377 0.0134 0.00649 0.2215 0.0644 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 15)
  ("the negation of the formula to be proved, the proofs that result are not a natural" 0.1812 0.8731 0.2439 0.2573 0.2573 0.0134 0.00717 0.2058 0.0266 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 15)
  ("sequence of easily justifiable steps. Likewise, very few proofs in mathematics are" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2635 0.2769 0.2769 0.0134 0.00774 0.2642 0.0729 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 11)
  ("from axioms directly. Mathematicians in practice usually reason in a more flexible" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2831 0.2965 0.2965 0.0134 0.00654 0.3112 0.0388 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 11)
  ("way." 0.1812 0.2173 0.3028 0.3162 0.3162 0.0134 nil 0.2082 0.0361 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 0)
  ("Natural deduction is a deductive system in which logical reasoning is expressed" 0.2282 0.8730 0.3224 0.3358 0.3358 0.0134 0.00463 0.3048 0.0643 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 11)
  ("by inference rules closely related to the “natural” way of reasoning. This contrasts" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3420 0.3554 0.3554 0.0134 0.00637 0.2279 0.0202 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 12)
  ("with Hilbert systems, which instead use axiom rules as much as possible to express" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3616 0.3750 0.3750 0.0134 0.00606 0.3019 0.0372 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("the logical laws of deductive reasoning." 0.1812 0.5233 0.3812 0.3947 0.3947 0.0134 0.00949 0.2212 0.0266 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 5)
  ("Natural deduction in its modern form" 0.5411 0.8730 0.3812 0.3947 0.3947 0.0134 0.00949 0.2535 0.0643 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 5)
  ("was independently proposed by the German mathematician Gerhard Gentzen in" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4009 0.4143 0.4143 0.0134 0.00854 0.2513 0.0304 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 9)
  ("1934. In Gentzen’s natural deductive system, a formula is represented by a set A" 0.1812 0.8729 0.4205 0.4339 0.4339 0.0134 0.00719 0.2419 0.0436 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 13)
  ("of formulas, A = {A_{1}, A_{2}, ..., A_{n}}, where the comma in A are understood as ∨. To" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4385 0.4548 0.4536 0.0134 0.00666 0.3578 0.0154 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 17)
  ("avoid the confusion, we will write A as" 0.1812 0.5055 0.4597 0.4732 0.4732 0.0134 0.00637 0.3110 0.0441 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 7)
  ("(A_{1} ​| A_{2} ​| · · · | A_{n})." 0.4473 0.6068 0.4929 0.5092 0.5080 0.0151 0.00542 0.4520 0.0287 "BRIFLF+CMSY10" 0.0151 nil t 8)
  ("A is called a sequent by Gentzen. If n = 0, i.e., A = ( ), which is equivalent to ⊥" 0.1812 0.8729 0.5276 0.5427 0.5427 0.0134 0.00713 0.3018 0.0143 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 20)
  ("or (⊥). Since we assume that A is a set, duplicated formulas are removed and the" 0.1812 0.8730 0.5472 0.5623 0.5623 0.0134 0.00666 0.2828 0.0170 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 15)
  ("order of formulas in A is irrelevant." 0.1812 0.4768 0.5684 0.5818 0.5818 0.0134 0.00637 0.2910 0.0436 "CQDOTO+CMR12" 0.0151 nil nil 6)
  ("Definition 3.2.8. A natural deductive system G consists of the following three in-" 0.1812 0.8729 0.5990 0.6141 0.6141 0.0134 0.00698 0.2779 0.0953 "HYRIRB+CMTI12" 0.0151 nil t 12)
  ("ference rules, plus the following logical rules which guide the introduction and elim-" 0.1812 0.8730 0.6203 0.6337 0.6337 0.0134 0.00649 0.2604 0.0579 "HYRIRB+CMTI12" 0.0151 nil t 12)
  ("ination of logical operators in the sequents." 0.1812 0.5397 0.6399 0.6533 0.6533 0.0134 0.00684 0.2121 0.0591 "HYRIRB+CMTI12" 0.0151 nil t 6)
